// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #5944 NEWRULE: ProxyExecution with ConfigurationRemotingServer (DSCourier)
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/5944
// Pull request author: unresolvedhost
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential WinGet Proxy Execution via DSC Engine
// Sigma ID: 3b7e1f2a-d4c9-4e80-b5a3-f9c2e1d08745
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Richard Roy (@unresolvedhost)
// Date: 2026-10-21 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1218, execution, T1059.001
// Description: Detects WindowsPackageManagerServer.exe (WinGet) spawning ConfigurationRemotingServer.exe with
//   PowerShell in the command line. HackTools such as DSCourier abuse WinGet's ability to apply DSC
//   configurations to proxy code execution — a malicious DSC YAML causes WinGet to invoke
//   ConfigurationRemotingServer.exe, which runs PowerShell to process and execute the configuration
//   payload.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate DSC configurations executed via WinGet
// References:
//   - https://dylansec.com/DSCourier/
//   - https://github.com/DylanDavis1/DSCourier
//   - https://eclipsesec.com/posts/DSCourier/
//   - https://www.zerosalarium.com/2024/12/LOLBIN%20WinGet%20execute%20PowerShell%20script.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/2a59da406ad583a51d36303ca8016be7ca38c224/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_winget_proxy_execution_via_dsc.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\WindowsPackageManagerServer.exe" and FolderPath endswith "\\ConfigurationRemotingServer.exe" and ProcessCommandLine contains "pwsh"
